{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-ad91efbbcec7094d", "type": "tool_result", "content": "500\tEvaluation Methods\n501\t\n502\tFor evaluating Qwen-Audio-3.0-TTS’s zero-shot speech generation capabilities, we focus on three\n503\tkey aspects: content consistency, speaker similarity, and audio quality. For content consistency, we\n504\tmeasure the Character Error Rate (CER) or Word Error Rate (WER) of the ASR transcription against\n505\tthe given text, using Whisper-large V3 [28] for English ASR and Paraformer [29, 30] for Chinese\n506\tASR. To assess speaker similarity, we extract speaker embeddings from the generated speech using\n507\tthe ERes2Net speaker verification model [31] and calculate the cosine similarity with the embedding\n508\tof the reference speech. For audio quality, we score the generated speech using the DNSMOS\n509\tnetwork [32], the scores of which show high correlations with human auditory perception.\n510\tOur core evaluations use SEED-TTS-Eval [2] and an extended CV3-Eval [4] covering seven additional languages. SEED-TTS-Eval reports both ERes2Net and WavLM similarity for comparison\n511\twith prior work [33].\n512\tWe compare Qwen-Audio-3.0-TTS with widely used or competitive speech generation models.\n513\tNon-autoregressive (NAR) baselines include F5-TTS [8], F5R-TTS [34], and LongCat-AudioDiT\n514\t[35]. Autoregressive (AR) baselines include Seed-TTS [2], FireRedTTS-2 [36], IndexTTS2 [37],\n515\tQwen2.5-Omni [38], Qwen3.5-Omni [39], Qwen3-TTS [6], Minimax-Speech [40], CosyVoice3 [4],\n516\tDots.TTS [12], and VoxCPM2 [13].\n517\tBeyond these test sets, we introduce Qwen-Audio-TTS-Eval, a diagnostic benchmark for\n518\tdeployment-oriented speech generation, consisting of the following evaluation dimensions:\n519\t• Text Normalization: 1,375 Chinese and English cases containing non-standard words, including\n520\tnumbers, dates, currencies, abbreviations, codes, formulas, and symbols, testing whether models\n521\tcan verbalize them correctly.\n522\t• Long-form Speech Generation: 200 paragraph-level Chinese and English cases, typically producing utterances of 1.5–3 minutes, evaluating content consistency, speaker consistency, and\n523\tprosodic stability in one-pass generation. English cases are adapted from [41] with same-speaker\n524\treference utterances, while Chinese cases are curated in-house.\n525\t• Acoustic Robustness: 894 Chinese and English cases with noisy, reverberant, and unclear prompt\n526\tspeech, testing robustness to degraded enrollment audio.\n527\t• Instruction Following under Zero-shot Voice Cloning: 440 cases covering single-attribute control of emotion, speech rate, and volume, as well as multi-attribute instructions expressed in natural language or structured key-value formats.\n528\tWe also evaluate transfer from the pretrained model to speaker-fine-tuned models. Task-specific\n529\tbenchmark, calibration, and annotation protocols are reported alongside the corresponding results\n530\tbelow.\n531\t\n532\t4\n533\t\n534\tExperimental Results\n535\t\n536\t4.1\n537\t\n538\tAblation of the Speech Tokenizer\n539\t\n540\tWe investigate the impact of frame rate and codebook size on the tokenizer across automatic speech\n541\trecognition (ASR) and downstream text-to-speech (TTS) tasks.\n542\tIntrinsic ASR results on Common Voice [42] and FLEURS [43], reported in Table 1, show that increasing the codebook size recovers the performance loss caused by reducing the frame rate. Table 2\n543\t8\n544\t\n545\t\fshows the same trend: reducing the frame rate from 25 to 12.5 Hz with the same 6,561-code vocabulary degrades content consistency and speaker similarity, whereas codebook scaling recovers the\n546\tloss. Among the 12.5 Hz variants, the 59,049-code configuration achieves the best content consistency, while the 19,683-code configuration retains marginally higher speaker similarity, motivating\n547\tthe final accuracy–similarity–rate trade-off.\n548\tTable 1: ASR error rates (%) on Common Voice (CV) and FLEURS. CER is used for Chinese,\n549\tJapanese, and Korean; WER is used for English. The best 12.5 Hz result is bold.\n550\tTokenizer\n551\tCosyVoice3\n552\tQwen-Audio-3.0-TTS\n553\tQwen-Audio-3.0-TTS\n554\tQwen-Audio-3.0-TTS\n555\t\n556\tCodebook\n557\t\n558\tRate\n559\t\n560\tCV-zh\n561\t\n562\tCV-en\n563\t\n564\tCV-ja\n565\t\n566\tCV-ko\n567\t\n568\tFLEURS-zh\n569\t\n570\tFLEURS-en\n571\t\n572\t6,561\n573\t6,561\n574\t19,683\n575\t59,049\n576\t\n577\t25 Hz\n578\t12.5 Hz\n579\t12.5 Hz\n580\t12.5 Hz\n581\t\n582\t10.63\n583\t11.23\n584\t10.79\n585\t10.24\n586\t\n587\t13.07\n588\t15.40\n589\t13.39\n590\t12.52\n591\t\n592\t15.61\n593\t18.68\n594\t16.63\n595\t15.21\n596\t\n597\t11.35\n598\t13.22\n599\t11.45\n600\t11.70\n601\t\n602\t3.77\n603\t4.18\n604\t4.00\n605\t3.85\n606\t\n607\t5.43\n608\t5.33\n609\t4.91\n610\t4.69\n611\t\n612\tTable 2: Zero-shot TTS performance of different tokenizers on SEED-TTS-Eval. Content consistency is measured by CER/WER, and speaker similarity (SIM) is measured by ERes2Net and\n613\treported as a percentage. The best 12.5 Hz result in each column is shown in bold.\n614\tTokenizer\n615\t\n616\tCodebook\n617\t\n618\tFrame Rate\n619\t\n620\ttest-zh\n621\t\n622\tSize\n623\tCosyVoice3\n624\tQwen-Audio-3.0-TTS\n625\tQwen-Audio-3.0-TTS\n626\tQwen-Audio-3.0-TTS\n627\t\n628\t4.2\n629\t\n630\t6,561\n631\t6,561\n632\t19,683\n633\t59,049\n634\t\n635\t25 Hz\n636\t12.5 Hz\n637\t12.5 Hz\n638\t12.5 Hz\n639\t\n640\ttest-en\n641\t\n642\ttest-hard\n643\t\n644\tCER (%) ↓\n645\t\n646\tSIM (%) ↑\n647\t\n648\tWER (%) ↓\n649\t\n650\tSIM (%) ↑\n651\t\n652\tCER (%) ↓\n653\t\n654\tSIM (%) ↑\n655\t\n656\t1.45\n657\t2.59\n658\t1.48\n659\t1.23\n660\t\n661\t80.60\n662\t72.44\n663\t83.25\n664\t83.09\n665\t\n666\t2.57\n667\t3.21\n668\t2.56\n669\t2.37\n670\t\n671\t73.60\n672\t61.64\n673\t77.58\n674\t77.49\n675\t\n676\t6.83\n677\t7.94\n678\t6.70\n679\t6.68\n680\t\n681\t77.60\n682\t69.78\n683\t80.85\n684\t80.6