designed with built-in robustness to degraded enrollment speech, without relying on a dedicated inference-time denoising mode. As shown in Table 9, this capability\n1571\tleads to strong results across all three conditions. We evaluate both standard and denoising modes\n1572\tfor MiniMax-Speech-2.8-HD and ElevenLabs-v3. Enabling denoising raises MiniMax’s DNSMOS\n1573\tfrom 3.464 to 3.728 on Noisy and from 3.065 to 3.343 on Reverb, but reduces SIM from 66.72 to\n1574\t63.83 and from 61.56 to 56.53, respectively. A similar trade-off appears for ElevenLabs-v3, whose\n1575\tdenoising mode improves Reverb DNSMOS from 3.090 to 4.025 and WER from 1.75% to 0.58%,\n1576\twhile its SIM remains low at 44.39%. By comparison, Qwen-Audio-3.0-TTS reaches DNSMOS\n1577\tscores of 3.962 and 3.925 on Noisy and Reverb, close to ElevenLabs-v3·Denoise, while achieving\n1578\tmuch higher SIM scores of 76.14% and 74.12%. On Reverb, it further obtains the best SIM together\n1579\twith the second-best WER and DNSMOS, showing a strong balance among denoising quality, intelligibility, and speaker preservation.\n1580\t12\n1581\t\n1582\t\fTable 9: Objective zero-shot TTS results under noisy, reverberant, and unclear prompt conditions.\n1583\tModels marked with “Denoise” use their inference-time denoising mode. Content error, ERes2Net\n1584\tspeaker similarity (SIM), and DNSMOS are reported. Bold and underlined values denote the best\n1585\tand second-best results in each column.\n1586\tNoisy\n1587\t\n1588\tModel\n1589\t\n1590\tReverb\n1591\t\n1592\tUnclear\n1593\t\n1594\tWER (%) ↓ SIM (%) ↑ DNSMOS ↑ WER (%) ↓ SIM (%) ↑ DNSMOS ↑ WER (%) ↓ SIM (%) ↑ DNSMOS ↑\n1595\tCommercial API Models\n1596\tMiniMax-Speech-2.8-HD\n1597\tMiniMax-Speech-2.8-HD·Denoise\n1598\tElevenLabs-v3\n1599\tElevenLabs-v3·Denoise\n1600\t\n1601\t0.85\n1602\t0.83\n1603\t1.17\n1604\t1.19\n1605\t\n1606\t66.72\n1607\t63.83\n1608\t46.91\n1609\t46.07\n1610\t\n1611\t3.464\n1612\t3.728\n1613\t3.779\n1614\t3.981\n1615\t\n1616\t0.83\n16