{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-aee3053e71f3a555", "type": "tool_result", "content": "2000\tIn this report, we present Qwen-Audio-3.0-TTS, a multilingual, freely controllable and highly robust\n2001\tspeech synthesis system oriented towards production deployment. Its 12.5 Hz speech tokenizer,\n2002\ttogether with chunk-based flow-matching and causal vocoder, reduces end-to-end latency. Joint\n2003\tLM–FM training and LM reinforcement learning deliver a strong balance of content consistency\n2004\tand naturalness. Acoustic robustness training and FM reinforcement learning progressively improve\n2005\tspeaker similarity, audio fidelity and robustness. Qwen-Audio-3.0-TTS achieves state-of-the-art\n2006\tperformance on many reported dimensions or the strongest aggregate scores across SEED-TTSEval, CV3-Eval, instruction-following, long-form, and adverse-prompt evaluations. It also ranks\n2007\tfirst on the independent Artificial Analysis Text-to-Speech Arena leaderboard released on July 16,\n2008\t2026.\n2009\tBeyond individual benchmark gains, Qwen-Audio-3.0-TTS advances TTS toward a unified and\n2010\tpractical speech-generation system. A single model integrates multilingual and multi-dialect synthesis, zero-shot voice cloning, free-style instruction following, fine-grained inline control, one-pass\n2011\tlong-form generation and robustness to degraded real-world prompts. In addition, we propose a\n2012\tscalable speaker adaptation protocol based on Qwen-Audio-3.0-TTS. We believe this provides a\n2013\tpractical foundation for the next generation of general-purpose, controllable, and deployment-ready\n2014\tTTS systems.\n2015\t\n2016\tAcknowledgements\n2017\tWe thank Bangduo Chen, Biao Tian, Bin Ma, Bin Yuan, Binbin Zhang, Chaohong Tan, Chen Ding,\n2018\tChong Deng, Chongde Zhang, Gang Qiao, Hongzhi Cai, Jianwei Yu, Jiaqi Shi, Jiaqing Liu, Jixing\n2019\tYu, Junhao Xu, Lingyun Zuo, Menglin Wu, Qian Chen, Sitong Zhao, Xian Yang, Yajie Wen, Yang\n2020\tBai, Yiping Peng, Yuting Teng, Ze Xu, Zhenglin Wang, Zhifu Gao, Ziyi Cheng for their valuable\n2021\t15\n2022\t\n2023\t\fcontributions to data curation, system development, evaluation, and infrastructure support. Names\n2024\tare listed alphabetically by given name.\n2025\t\n2026\t16\n2027\t\n2028\t\fReferences\n2029\t[1] Chengyi Wang, Sanyuan Chen, Yu Wu, Ziqiang Zhang, Long Zhou, Shujie Liu, Zhuo Chen,\n2030\tYanqing Liu, Huaming Wang, Jinyu Li, Lei He, Sheng Zhao, and Furu Wei. Neural codec\n2031\tlanguage models are zero-shot text to speech synthesizers. CoRR, abs/2301.02111, 2023.\n2032\t[2] Philip Anastassiou, Jiawei Chen, Jitong Chen, Yuanzhe Chen, Zhuo Chen, Ziyi Chen, Jian\n2033\tCong, Lelai Deng, Chuang Ding, Lu Gao, Mingqing Gong, Peisong Huang, Qingqing Huang,\n2034\tZhiying Huang, Yuanyuan Huo, Dongya Jia, Chumin Li, Feiya Li, Hui Li, Jiaxin Li, Xiaoyang\n2035\tLi, Xingxing Li, Lin Liu, Shouda Liu, Sichao Liu, Xudong Liu, Yuchen Liu, Zhengxi Liu,\n2036\tLu Lu, Junjie Pan, Xin Wang, Yuping Wang, Yuxuan Wang, Zhen Wei, Jian Wu, Chao Yao,\n2037\tYifeng Yang, Yuanhao Yi, Junteng Zhang, Qidi Zhang, Shuo Zhang, Wenjie Zhang, Yang\n2038\tZhang, Zilin Zhao, Dejian Zhong, and Xiaobin Zhuang. Seed-tts: A family of high-quality\n2039\tversatile speech generation models. CoRR, abs/2406.02430, 2024.\n2040\t[3] Matthew Le, Apoorv Vyas, Bowen Shi, Brian Karrer, Leda Sari, Rashel Moritz, Mary\n2041\tWilliamson, Vimal Manohar, Yossi Adi, Jay Mahadeokar, and Wei-Ning Hsu. Voicebox: Textguided multilingual universal speech generation at scale. In NeurIPS, 2023.\n2042\t[4] Zhihao Du, Changfeng Gao, Yuxuan Wang, Fan Yu, Tianyu Zhao, Hao Wang, Xiang Lv, Hui\n2043\tWang, Chongjia Ni, Xian Shi, Keyu An, Guanrou Yang, Yabin Li, Yanni Chen, Zhifu Gao,\n2044\tQian Chen, Yue Gu, Mengzhe Chen, Yafeng Chen, Shiliang Zhang, Wen Wang, and Jieping\n2045\tYe. Cosyvoice 3: Towards in-the-wild speech generation via scaling-up and post-training,\n2046\t